> But proposed solution doesn't work, because the the `file` argument returns the base-name of the file, not the full path. But I did find an alternative solution, and am just pointing out your mistake.

There is no mistake here. The `file` argument in `config.use_file_extension` is the exact `name` that is supplied for `MiniIcons.get()`. For file icons it is [recommended to use full path instead of basename](https://github.com/echasnovski/mini.nvim/blob/3f71fd3573981bf18f76b2bfb6518c4ae728638d/doc/mini-icons.txt#L43-L45), specifically for this kind of solutions to be able to work.

You can verify that by explicitly executing `MiniIcons.get('file', '~/.config/hypr/hello.conf')` with the following setup and see entries of `:=_G.log`:

```lua
_G.log = {}
require('mini.icons').setup({
  use_file_extension = function(ext, file)
    table.insert(_G.log, { ext, file })
    return true
  end,
})
```

What you probably see is the effect of a plugin that either:
- Doesn't have direct 'mini.icons' integration and what you see is the result of 'nvim-web-devicons' mock (which *only* works with basenames, as this is how the plugin works).
- Has direct 'mini.icons' integration but uses basename instead of full path.

All 'mini.nvim' plugins use `MiniIcons.get()` properly.

---

If using exact file names is enough for you, then `use_file_extension` is too much here and using `file` directly is better for performance:

```lua
local hyperland_icon = { glyph = '', hl = 'MiniIconsCyan' }
require('mini.icons').setup({
  file = {
    ['animations.conf'] = hyperland_icon,
    ['hypridle.conf'] = hyperland_icon,
    ['hyprland.conf'] = hyperland_icon,
    ['hyprlock.conf'] = hyperland_icon,
  },
  extension = {
    conf = { glyph = '', hl = 'SettingsIconColor' },
  },
})
```
